Think of your immune system like a security team that protects your body from invaders like viruses and bacteria. Usually, this team needs to see the actual enemy to learn how to fight it, but by the time they figure out a defense, the enemy might have already caused you to get sick. Vaccines act as a "training exercise" for this security team, allowing them to practice without the risk of a real infection.

When you get a vaccine, it introduces a harmless piece of a virus or a weakened version of it into your body. This piece is like a wanted poster or a dummy target. Your immune system spots it and recognizes it as a threat, even though it can’t actually make you ill. This triggers your body to produce specific weapons called antibodies and to create memory cells that remember exactly what that specific invader looks like.

Once the training is over, the harmless piece is cleared out, but your immune system keeps those memory cells around. These cells are like security guards who have seen the wanted poster and are now on high alert. If the real, dangerous virus ever tries to enter your body in the future, your immune system doesn’t have to start from scratch. It immediately recognizes the invader and launches a fast, strong attack to stop it before
